DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aaggggrr driver implements IEEE 802.1AX (formerly 802.3ad) Link Aggrega‐
     tion for combining one or more Ethernet interfaces into a single logical
     interface.  The Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP) is used to nego‐
     tiate the aggregation of member interfaces with a partner device.

     aaggggrr interfaces must be configured to use one or more Ethernet interfaces
     as ports, and communicate with a partner device over those ports using
     LACP.  Member interfaces that negotiate with a partner device are aggre‐
     gated into a single logical link.  Packets transmitted through an aaggggrr
     interface select an aggregated interface for transmission.  Packets re‐
     ceived by an aggregated device appear to be received by the aaggggrr inter‐
     face it is a member of.

     aaggggrr interfaces can be created at runtime using the iiffccoonnffiigg aaggggrr_N ccrreeaattee
     command or by setting up a hostname.if(5) configuration file for
     netstart(8).  The interface itself can be configured with ifconfig(8);
     see its manual page for more information.

     A random MAC address is generated when an aaggggrr interface is created.  A
     specific MAC address can be assigned using the ifconfig(8) command llllaaddddrr
     _e_t_h_e_r_a_d_d_r.

     Other forms of aggregation of Ethernet interfaces are available using the
     trunk(4) driver.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Create an aaggggrr virtual interface on top of two physical interfaces:

           # ifconfig aggr0 create
           # ifconfig aggr0 trunkport em0
           # ifconfig aggr0 trunkport em1
           # ifconfig aggr0 10.1.1.100/24
           # ifconfig aggr0 up

